<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Radon in Regina is common enough that the City put out its own advisory in June 2024. It cites research in which "50% of homes tested in Regina exceeded 200 Bq/m3", and 200 Bq/m3 (becquerels per cubic metre, about 5.4 pCi/L if you're used to American numbers) is Health Canada's guideline. Neither figure tells you about your house. Only a test does.</p><p>Radon is a colourless, odourless gas from the ground that collects in basements. Health Canada calls it the number one cause of lung cancer in non-smokers, second only to smoking overall, and links it to more than 3,000 lung cancer deaths in Canada each year. The fix is fairly boring: <a href="/radon-testing/">run a long-term test</a>, and if the number is high, have a certified professional put in a system. Prairie Radon Co holds no certification itself; we put Regina homeowners in touch with independent radon professionals, and you can check any professional's certification in C-NRPP's online directory. Questions? Call now at (204) 555-0173.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Regina</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Regina Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Long-term radon testing</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Health Canada's standard is a test of at least 91 days in the lowest lived-in level of the house, ideally through the heating season. In a Regina home that usually means the finished basement: the kids' bedrooms, the rec room or the basement suite.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Radon mitigation</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A sub-slab depressurization system runs a pipe through the basement slab, and a fan vents the gas outside. Before quoting, a certified installer checks how air moves under your slab, because some Regina houses need more than one suction point.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Radon testing for a home sale</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">We found no Saskatchewan rule that requires a radon test at sale. If radon has come up in a Regina inspection or in the conditions, a test gives both sides a real number, and your realtor or lawyer can tell you how to write it in.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 8px;">Fan replacement and system checks</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A noisy fan, a dead one or a manometer reading that looks wrong means the system needs a look. Health Canada's 2014 national estimate for a replacement fan is $200 to $300; a Regina professional will quote your actual job.</p>
      </div>
</div>
<style>
